    desc: "At the Heian court, noblewomen dressed in weightless stacks of unlined silk: a kosode base, five itsutsuginu robes, uchigi layers, an uchikake outer drape and a tied karagoromo jacket over trailing nagabakama trousers — the 'twelve-layer' label is impressionistic, since counts varied with rank, wealth and season. The art was kasane no irome: color combinations naming snow, wisteria, plum and autumn grass, read through only a few centimeters of layered edge at collar and sleeve. Gowns flowed for metres past the body, faces floated behind standing silk screens, and The Tale of Genji reads like a catalogue of these color choices. The style fell from daily use after the court moved from Kyoto, but imperial household rites, Shinto kagura performance and modern museums kept the layering alive — it is still worn at imperial ceremonies. The look: pastel strata pooling like mountain mist around a still, seated figure.",
