    desc: "From the huge wood-chamber mounds of Gyeongju, capital of the Silla kingdom, excavators lifted gold crowns whose uprights grow like shamanic trees and branching antlers, hung with discs and curved gogok comma-shaped jades that catch light with every step. Matching gold girdles, caps with loops for feather holders, openwork earrings with granulated chains and glass-bead necklaces completed a burial ensemble meant to turn the dead king into a shining axis of the realm. The style fuses steppe and Siberian tree-of-life ideas carried along northeast Asian routes with local gold-beating mastery — sheets were hammered thin, cut, wired and riveted rather than cast. Tombs such as Geumgwanchong ('gold crown tomb'), Cheonmachong and Hwangnamdaechong gave their names to the finds inside them. Crowns and jewelry from these mounds are among Korea's National Treasures, displayed in Gyeongju and Seoul. The look: glimmering golden treeforms against dark mound earth.",
