    desc: "Goguryeo elites were buried in stone chambers under earth mounds, and their walls became the liveliest paint fields of ancient East Asia: early tombs show banquets, kitchens, wrestlers and hunters on galloping horses, while later chambers swirl with the four guardian spirits — azure dragon, white tiger, vermilion bird and black tortoise-serpent — set under painted star maps that turn the tomb into a cosmos. Mineral pigments on lime plaster over stone give the images a chalk-bright clarity that survived fifteen centuries of darkness. Landmark chambers include the dated Anak Tomb No. 3 (357 CE), the Hunting-scene chamber of Muyongchong and the Four Guardians chambers at Gangseo. China's Ji'an cluster and the Pyongyang group were inscribed by UNESCO in 2004 as the Complex of Koguryo Tombs. The dynamic figures fed directly into later Korean painting and temple iconography. The look: galloping silhouette-line drawing on chalky mineral color.",
