    n: "Bangjja yugi brassware", a: ["bangjja ritual brass vessels", "Korean hammered brassware", "yugi tableware"],
    cat: "Textile & Craft", c: "hammer-raised copper-tin alloy vessels", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Korean metal-casting guilds; Daegu workshops", g: "Daegu and nationwide temple and ritual use, South Korea",
    p: "Goryeo-Joseon workshop roots; codified as craft in Joseon; living today", era: "Joseon ritual-craft classic",
    sum: "Cast then hammer-forged bronze vessels — bangjja yugi tableware rings like a bell and serves ancestral rites across Korea.",
    desc: "Bangjja yugi begins as a cast copper-tin disc that a team of smiths heat, hammer and fold over and over — historically up to eleven smiths striking in rhythm around the hot billet — until repeated heating and hammering compress the alloy into dense, bell-resonant vessels. The result is tableware, ritual cups, stem bowls and water vessels whose surfaces take a soft satin glow and whose purity made them the standard for ancestral jesa offerings, temple meals and royal surasang tables. Joseon etiquette reserved hammered yugi for ritual and elite tables, deepening its ceremonial aura. Daegu became the craft's capital, and its workshops and the Daegu Bangjja Yugi Museum keep the tradition alive; masters are recognized as Intangible Cultural Heritage holders. The hammered surface is prized as hygienic, an old belief now echoed by antimicrobial studies of brass. The look: burnished olive-gold vessels with faint hammer clouds, resting light like stone.",
