    cat: "Textile & Craft", c: "knot-resist indigo dyeing", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Bai minority dyers of Zhoucheng", g: "Zhoucheng village, Dali, Yunnan, China",
    p: "technique documented Tang-era; Zhoucheng heyday Qing-to-present", era: "southwest China craft living tradition",
    sum: "In Zhoucheng village beneath Cangshan mountain, Bai dyers knot cotton and drop it into indigo vats, untying webs of butterflies and flowers in blue-on-white.",
    desc: "Zhoucheng, a Bai village on the Dali plain of Yunnan, is China's living capital of jiaoxie — the tie-and-bind resist dyeing described in Tang-dynasty records and preserved in the Shōsōin repository in Nara. Women stitch and twist cotton cloth over strings and beans, then dip it repeatedly in deep indigo vats fermented from local plants; the bound areas stay white while the ground deepens toward black-blue with each pass. Unbound, the cloth blossoms into radiating butterflies, fish, pomegranates and sunbursts edged by the soft halo of dye creep — the signature of true hand tying. Courtyard workshops sell tablecloths, scarves and hanging panels to travelers along the Tea Horse Road trade route that crosses Dali, and the craft is listed in provincial and national intangible heritage programs. The look: crystalline white blooms floating on saturated indigo fields.",
