    cat: "Textile & Craft", c: "backstrap-loom tapestry and embroidery", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Li (Hlai) women of Hainan", g: "Hainan island, China — Wuzhishan and Baisha highlands",
    p: "centuries-deep roots; UNESCO Urgent Safeguarding listing 2009", era: "island weaving living tradition",
    sum: "On backstrap looms, Li women of Hainan weave ancestors, frogs and cyclone hooks into dense indigo-and-madder brocade — cloth as genealogy.",
    desc: "The Li (Hlai) people of Hainan developed one of Asia's oldest continuous textile systems: hand-spun cotton spun, dyed with indigo and plant reds, woven on simple backstrap looms into tapestry brocades, then overlaid with fine double-faced embroidery. Motifs read like a clan archive — stylized human figures (ancestors and dancers), totemic frogs and water buffalo, diamond fields and hooked borders — and no two women's compositions match. Ceremonial longbei quilts, the grandest genre, layered these motifs for weddings and funerals. UNESCO placed the whole chain — spinning, dyeing, weaving and embroidering — on its List of Intangible Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding in 2009, as younger Hainanese drifted to city work. Museums in Haikou and the island's heritage villages now train new weavers around Wuzhishan. The look: inky geometric figures flickering over deep madder-and-indigo grounds.",
