    p: "pre-contact core; suppressed colonial-missionary era; 21st-century revival", era: "Bornean body-lore classic",
    sum: "Hand-tapped thorn-and-stick tattoos — aso' dragon-dogs, eggplant-blossom shoulder rosettes — mapped Bornean Dayak lives onto skin.",
    desc: "Across Borneo, Dayak and Iban communities developed a hand-tapped tattooing craft: bundles of needles set in a wooden haft, tapped with a stick, driven by lamp-black soot pigment, cutting motifs an eighth of an inch into skin. Kenyah and Kayan chests and shoulders carried the aso' — a coil-bodied dragon-dog that guarded its bearer — while Iban men earned the bungai terung, an eggplant-flower rosette at each shoulder that marked readiness for travel and headhunting, with further marks recording journeys, raids and river-borne rank. Women's forearms bore plant and prawn motifs tied to weaving skill. Missionaries and colonial bans pushed the practice underground for decades, but Bornean artists revived hand-tapping in the 2000s and museum collections re-contextualized tattooed figures and photographs. The look: dense black geometry blooming over brown skin, tapped line by trembling line.",
    col: [{ h: "#17120e", n: "lamp-black ink" }, { h: "#7a4a2e", n: "skin umber" }, { h: "#3f2d1d", n: "jungle shadow" }, { h: "#c9b98a", n: "bone-white haft" }, { h: "#8f2f23", n: "bead accent red" }],
