    cat: "Textile & Craft", c: "ritual warp-ikat hangings", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Iban master weavers (indu iban)", g: "Sarawak, Malaysian Borneo, and West Kalimantan",
    p: "centuries-old corpus; living tradition tied to Gawai rites", era: "Bornean ritual-textile classic",
    sum: "Dream-dyed warp ikat giants — pua kumbu cloths hang at Iban feasts as the river between human village and spirit world.",
    desc: "Among the Iban of Sarawak, the greatest textile a household owns is the pua kumbu: a hand-spun cotton hanging a metre wide and often two or more long, its warp tied and dyed in stages before weaving so crocodiles, hornbills, cobras and spirit figures swim across madder-red and indigo fields. Designs arrive in dreams, and the right to weave powerful patterns is earned in ranked steps over a lifetime — a misused design, elders warn, can make its weaver sick. The finished cloth demarcates ritual: it dresses the altar at Gawai harvest festivals, receives newborns, and once screened ritual spaces during headhunting feasts, its images mediating between the longhouse and the gods. Sarawak's museums and the Fowler collection in Los Angeles hold classic 19th-20th century examples, while weavers in the Batang Ai and Saribas rivers still tie new dreams. The look: hot madder red cut by inky spirit geometry.",
