    cat: "Textile & Craft", c: "noble warp-ikat blankets", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Sumbanese noble households", g: "Sumba island, East Nusa Tenggara, Indonesia",
    p: "long developing corpus; peak trade era 1800s-1900s; living today", era: "eastern Indonesian ikat classic",
    sum: "On Sumba, horses, skull trees and crocodiles gallop across indigo-and-madder ikat blankets — hinggi cloth is a man's rank made wearable.",
    desc: "A Sumbanese man of rank wraps himself in hinggi: broad warp-ikat shoulder-and-hip blankets whose repeating fields of horses, prancing deer, crocodiles, skull-tree andung poles and star rosettes announce clan, courage and wealth. Dyeing is the slow miracle — morinda-root madder for blood reds and repeated indigo dips for blue-black grounds, with cream reserved by binding; motifs borrowed and transformed from Indian patola trade cloths weave beside purely Sumbanese imagery. Blankets are exchanged in staggering numbers at funerals, knit clans to ancestors, and once functioned as currency for horses and buffalo. Women wear the matched lau tube skirt, and the Pasola horseback ritual still fills dry-season fields with riders in red. Museum collections from Jakarta to Los Angeles document the corpus, and east Sumba's village workshops keep both looms and dye vats running. The look: bone-white riders stampeding across midnight indigo.",
    col: [{ h: "#131c2c", n: "deep indigo ground" }, { h: "#8e2a1e", n: "morinda blood red" }, { h: "#e4d7b8", n: "bound cream white" }, { h: "#5e7046", n: "field green accent" }, { h: "#c2792a", n: "ochre tan" }],
