    cat: "Religious & Sacred Art", c: "temple narrative painting on cloth", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Kamasan village painters of Klungkung", g: "Kamasan, Klungkung, Bali, Indonesia",
    p: "documented by the 16th-18th centuries; Kerta Gosa ceilings 18th-19th c.; living today", era: "pre-colonial Balinese court classic",
    sum: "Flat gold-veiled puppet figures crowd Kamasan cloths and temple ceilings — Bali's oldest continuous painting school still paints epics in Klungkung.",
    desc: "Kamasan painters served the courts of Klungkung with narrative cloths — langse hangings for pavilions, ider-ider strips for temple eaves, calendars and astrological charts — executed in a strictly flattened idiom derived from shadow-puppet projection: three-quarter profiles, angular limb bends, fine ink contour, earth-red and ochre fills, and prada gold leaf laid over court robes and borders. Scenes come from the Ramayana and Mahabharata, from the Tantri fables and from law and star-lore, read like comic strips by villagers gathered beneath them. The famous ceilings of the Kerta Gosa hall of justice at Klungkung layer karmic punishment and epic panels into a swallowing dome of this style. Painters' families in Kamasan village continue the school today, and museum collections from Ubud to Leiden preserve the classic cloths. The look: brittle gold-and-ochre puppet theater pinned flat to cotton.",
    col: [{ h: "#c8a24b", n: "prada gold leaf" }, { h: "#8e4a2a", n: "ochre flesh" }, { h: "#2a2a26", n: "ink contour black" }, { h: "#7c3226", n: "court red" }, { h: "#4b5d43", n: "forest green" }],
