    sum: "Two masts, seven sails, hull shaped by eye and prayer — the Bugis pinisi carried the last great sail-borne trade of the Indonesian archipelago.",
    desc: "In South Sulawesi shipyards like Bira, the Konjo and Bugis boatwrights build pinisi: sharp-prowed cargo sailing ships whose modern two-masted, seven-sail rig matured in the early 1900s out of older padewakang hulls that had carried spice-route trade for centuries. Hulls are raised plank by plank without drawings — builders shape by eye, ritual and inherited measure, launching with offerings to the sea, and the ships subsequently worked the entire archipelago, from Maluku copra to Bugis trade diaspora ports. Engine conversions changed the decks but not the craft: UNESCO inscribed 'Pinisi, art of boatbuilding in South Sulawesi' on its Intangible Cultural Heritage list in 2017, and Paotere harbor in Makassar still fills with masts. Contemporary phinisi now serve luxury charters alongside working freight. The look: black-tarred hulls, cream sails, raked masts against equatorial squall light.",
