    p: "form codified 1600s under Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal; rebuilt continuously", era: "17th-century Himalayan fortress classic",
    sum: "Tapering white walls, gold roofs, cantilever bridges — the dzong is at once fortress, monastery and town hall of every Bhutanese valley.",
    desc: "Every major Bhutanese valley is governed by a dzong: a massive complex of inward-sloping whitewashed stone and rammed-earth walls, ringed by a moat or river confluence, entered over cantilever bridges, its courtyards (dochey) stacking monk halls, government offices and temples around a central tower, the utse. The form crystallized under Zhabdrung Ngawang Namgyal in the 1600s as he unified Bhutan, at sites like Punakha Dzong (1637) at the Mo Chhu-Pho Chhu confluence and Trongsa, the largest, strung along a ridge throat. Gilt roofs, painted timber cornices, murals and guardian statues layer sacred function over military logic; dzongs also host annual tsechu festivals when whole districts climb their ramps. Bhutan's UNESCO tentative list names dzongs as centers of authority, and the buildings remain living seats of both monastic and civil power. The look: sheer white taper against green gorge, red roof-band, gold ridge line.",
