    sum: "On backstrap looms, Bhutanese women weave thagzo — silk-and-cotton patterned cloth dense enough that a kira can carry a whole village's motif memory.",
    desc: "Thagzo, the Bhutanese art of weaving, turns the humble backstrap loom into a national atelier: weavers tension warps around their waists and raise pattern by hand-picked supplementary wefts, producing cloths of astonishing density — silk kushuthara brocade kiras from Bumthang and Lhuentse that shimmer with intricate zigzag and star fields, woolen mathra winter cloth, and named pattern families passed down by generation. The woven arts dress the nation: every Bhutanese wears the gho robe or kira dress as mandated national costume, and gifted textiles rank among the country's highest honors, exchanged at weddings, promotions and royal audiences. The Royal Textile Academy and its museum in Thimphu document techniques and train weavers, while eastern villages remain the production heartland. The look: jewel-toned geometry woven so tight it reads like embroidery across a matte cotton ground.",
