    p: "Malla-era flowering 14th-18th centuries; living guild tradition", era: "Malla-period valley classic",
    sum: "Sal-wood deities lean from every strut, lintel and lattice of the Kathmandu Valley — Newar carvers turned temples into inside-out woodblock prints.",
    desc: "The Newar towns of the Kathmandu Valley are forests of carved timber: roof struts (tundal) carry full-figure deities gazing down from eaves, doorways sit beneath multi-lobed torana lintels crawling with Garuda and makara, and the famous lattice windows — like the Peacock Window of Bhaktapur's Pujari Math — pierce monastery walls with lace-fine openwork. Under the Malla kings, competing city courts fueled a golden age in which carver guilds refined a canon of deities, erotic struts, donor portraits and guardian masks, the craft passed through hereditary shilpakar families. Sal hardwood is carved in deep, shadow-catching relief, oiled dark, and rebuilt after the 1934 and 2015 earthquakes by masters and apprentices alike. UNESCO inscribed the Kathmandu Valley's monument zones in 1979, and Patan's woodcarving workshops keep chisels moving. The look: near-black relief so deep it reads as sculpture, throwing temple-morning shadows.",
