    n: "Tibetan mani stones", a: ["mani stone carvings", "Om Mani Padme Hum walls", "prayer stone heaps"],
    cat: "Religious & Sacred Art", c: "mantra-carved devotional stone", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Tibetan Buddhist pilgrims and carvers", g: "Tibetan plateau — Kham/Amdo and the Himalayan belt",
    p: "mantra practice from c. 1000 CE onward; heaps grow continuously today", era: "plateau devotional living tradition",
    sum: "Boulders and slabs carved with Om Mani Padme Hum pile into walls and hills across the plateau — mani stones are prayers you can walk through.",
    desc: "Wherever Tibetan Buddhism travels, hands carve mani stones: slate slabs and river boulders incised with the six-syllable mantra Om Mani Padme Hum, with Avalokiteshvara, protector deities and relief sutras, then painted in mineral red, white and blue. Devout travelers add a carved stone at passes, springs and temple gates, so single offerings accrete into mani walls, cairns and hill-sized heaps — Yushu's Gyanak Mani holds hundreds of millions of carved stones in a ridge-length heap of prayer. Carvers work by commission and merit, passing stylized letterforms down through families; weather repaints the stones in lichen and mineral wash between generations of re-painting. The heaps function as scripture at landscape scale — walking a mani wall clockwise is itself a recitation. Himalayan museums and sites from Ladakh to Kham preserve classic walls. The look: slate-grey masses flickering with pigment in carved valleys of stone.",
    col: [{ h: "#6e7276", n: "slate grey" }, { h: "#a63a2b", n: "mantra red" }, { h: "#e9e5da", n: "whitewash prayer white" }, { h: "#31518f", n: "sky-syllable blue" }, { h: "#5d6b4f", n: "lichen green" }],
